  "response": "Apportioning responsibility for an injurious contact between a collaborative robot and a human worker begins with an exact reconstruction of the cell as it stood at the moment of the event, since the engineering record, and not the parties' narratives, fixes both the mode of operation and the protective measures then in force. The collaborative robot was operating in a power and force limiting mode at the time of the contact, and a safety laser scanner defined the protective field surrounding the robot's workspace; a genuinely force-limited mode of operation and a scanner-delimited protective field cannot both have been in effect in that cell. The applicable performance criterion must be stated with equal care: ISO/TS 15066 prohibits any physical contact between a collaborative robot and a human worker, and such a blanket prohibition cannot be squared with the force-limited mode under which the machine was running when the worker was struck, so the prohibition and that mode of operation cannot both stand.\n\nA task-based risk assessment of the workcell was required before the robot was placed into production, an obligation generated by ISO 10218-2, which governs the integration, installation, and commissioning of a robot system within a workcell. Collaborative robot applications are exempt from the risk assessment requirements of ISO 12100, and that exemption cannot be reconciled with the pre-production assessment obligation just described; one or the other must give way, for both cannot be true of the same installation.\n\nThe manufacturer attributed the incident to the plant's alteration of the robot's safety configuration. The plant operator attributed the incident to a defect in the robot's force-monitoring firmware. Adjudication therefore depends on artifacts that survive the event rather than on either party's reconstruction of it. Firmware version records identify which software release was executing at the moment of contact. Checksum comparison of the stored safety configuration file reveals unauthorized parameter changes. Logged firmware identifiers, configuration checksums, safety-signal traces and maintenance records together allow the investigator to distinguish what the control software did from what the integrator and the maintenance crew did to the cell around it.\n\nAt least one of two failures is thereby established, and perhaps both: the robot's control software failed to limit contact force to the configured threshold, or the workcell installation deviated from the manufacturer's integration requirements. Even though the plant locates the origin of the event in defective force-monitoring firmware, the unauthorized parameter changes exposed by the comparison of configuration checksums are what demonstrate the second of these, namely the departure of the as-built cell from the integration requirements specified by the manufacturer. The protective stop did not activate before the worker was struck; in other words, the robot failed to halt its motion prior to making contact with the worker, and that failure to halt was brought about by the installation's deviation from the manufacturer's integration requirements.\n\nThe practical consequence for such disputes is that neither attribution can be credited on its own terms. Only the physical and digital residue of the cell at the moment of contact, read against the integration duties that the standards impose on whoever commissioned the application, determines whether the fault lies in the sensing and control chain or in the practices that surrounded it.",
